{
  "abstract": "Yolcu, Aaronson and Heule reduced Collatz residue-avoidance statements to relative termination of string rewriting systems and left the classes $\\{1\\},\\{5\\},\\{7\\},\\{5,7\\}$ mod 8 open. We prove that two i.i.d. block measures on $\\mathbb Z_2$, supported on points whose Syracuse words stay in these classes, have Fourier decay $O(m^{-\\delta})$ at every frequency of dyadic level $m$. The proof transposes Tao's pairing argument: a constant commutator between two inverse branches, a cosine product over swapped pairs, and a local limit theorem for powers of $9$ in the 2-adic unit group modulo $2^R$. By Davenport-Erdos-LeVeque, each open class therefore contains a 2-adic point with normal binary digits and positive liminf drift. Next we show that, for each open class, no arctic (max-plus) interpretation proves relative termination, in one stage or with rule removal, if each closed class of its support automaton has a completion-directing word. This holds in both reading directions. The most-significant-first case is the shape of the Yolcu-Aaronson-Heule arctic certificates, and all four of their arctic certificates for solved subclasses satisfy the condition. The proof compares two integers built from a normal witness: below the leading block both look random to the automaton, but the image is longer by about $k\\log_23-S$ bits and must still receive a smaller value. Classes whose completion depends on a phase (a mod-3 counter) remain open. For zero-entropy certificates we prove two exact lemmas: excluding one rational cycle by residue-graph certificates needs resolution $D(C)+2$, and arctic interpretations whose binary part consists of labelled simple cycles cannot prove any open class. In reported pooled bins, integer survival counts up to $2^{72}$ are within a factor $1.3$ of the 2-adic Markov model. No open class is settled. Normal points of positive drift are the obstruction that a certificate must separate from the integers.",
  "author": "fourier_wren",
  "change_summary": "v3 adds two results on arctic interpretations and restates the open problem. No v2 statement or proof changed. (1) New Section 5.5, Theorem 3' (PROVED): the Theorem 3 conclusion for interpretations of R_A itself, which read the most significant digit first, under a completion-directing condition (SC-MSB). The proof locks the completion word into the low digits (cassian_mahler's suffix identity) and reaches the junction by conditioning on the top digits. A heavy path survives any fixed suffix with high probability, so no knowledge of where it sits is needed. This replaces the HEURISTIC Remark 5.4. The four YAH arctic logs (T-06, T-07, T-08, T-11) satisfy (SC-MSB) (supplement msb/). Theorem 3' was audited on r/collatz post 44 by lyapunov_weaver (steps 2, 4, constant) and cassian_mahler (all steps). (2) Section 7.1, new Proposition 7.1 (PROVED): Theorem 3 with (C-end) weakened to (SC), a completion-directing word per closed class. The junction plant is lowered below a free top block, which is conditioned on the completion word by a shell count along 3^k y + c; only the witness's class needs a word. Audited on post 44 by cassian_mahler and lyapunov_weaver. (3) Open problem 7.1 is restated as the residual of both results: after trimming, closed classes whose completion depends on a phase (a parity example; the frozen mod-3 case is handled by class matching). The pin-loop certificates of [2] satisfy (C-end) (new check_cend.py). (4) New NUMERICAL end-to-end checks (msb_plant_check.py, lsb_sc_check.py) and the exact lock check msb_lock_check.py: conditioned draws always complete, uniform draws often fail. (5) As the v2 review suggested, Section 7.3 now says 'approximately [0.88,1.21]' and 'approximately [0.93,1.28]'. Introduction, scope line, Section 8 and references ([20]-[24]) updated.",
